module gunbc.serving.home_serving_candidate_field

// WHICH CITED ADD-IN CARD, AT WHICH APPLIED POWER LIMIT, FOR A HOME SERVING BOX
// (DESIGN.md §3d; dag/gunbc/plans/p4_home_serving_power_semantics.dag, option C).
//
// THE ONE FOLD. This module owns the question, the funded axis, the constraint and the policy. It
// owns no catalog fact and no watt semantics:
//   - the candidate field comes from gunbc.serving.home_serving_accelerator_field, whose frontier
//     names this fold as its consumer ("the add-in list is the P4 purchase-candidate field; the SXM
//     list is reference-only"). Consuming BOTH lists is what retires that frontier: delete either
//     and this module stops resolving, so the lists are load-bearing rather than inert. §3c is
//     explicit that "a list of things" is not a contribution and a witness asserting the lists
//     exist does not close it; the fold that consumes them does.
//   - every candidate figure is read off an extdeps.gpu.nvidia row. This module cites nothing.
//   - the meaning of a recorded watt comes from gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy. The
//     candidate is the option-C PAIR — cited catalog TDP and planned applied limit, kept distinct
//     and related by that module's rule — and a pair becomes a candidate only by passing that
//     module's public entry. So "a 300 W card asked to run at 600 W" is not filtered out late, it
//     is not writable at all.
//
// WHY THE CEILINGS SIT WHERE THEY SIT (§3d: "a ceiling must exclude before Pareto, and the same
// Watt cannot be both without saying which"). Two ceilings, placed differently on purpose:
//  - THE BUYER'S PSU CEILING is a std.decision HardConstraint on the funded axis. It is genuinely
//    universal — it applies to every candidate alike — which is the precondition for expressing it
//    as a kernel constraint: scan_feasibility tests EVERY constraint against EVERY entry
//    (constraint_definite_infeasibilities), so a constraint may only carry a bound that is meant
//    for all of the field. A violating entry is EXCLUDED, never ranked.
//  - EACH CARD'S CITED TDP is NOT a kernel constraint, and this is the trap this module had to
//    avoid. Putting four per-card TDP bounds on one shared axis would let the 600 W card's bound
//    condemn the 300 W cards' entries — and, because the scan is universal, would condemn the 600 W
//    card's own 400 W and 600 W entries too, with causes that name the wrong constraint. A per-card
//    ceiling is therefore enforced where it is a fact about one pair: at minting, by
//    gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy. Exclusion by typed refusal at construction, and
//    exclusion by the kernel for the one bound that is universal. No second decision algebra is
//    added either way (§3), and every decision-shaped refusal this fold can produce is an arm of
//    std.decision::select_realization.
//
// WHAT THE FOLD DELIBERATELY DOES NOT RANK ON.
//  - Throughput, TTFT, tokens/second. The warm-capacity ledger row
//    (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.benchmark_warm_capacity_violation_read_as_engine_performance)
//    caps this fold at "consumes ONLY WarmValid as a funded axis, never a raw gpu-bench rate", and no
//    WarmValid is obtainable on this head: gunbc.serving.gpu_bench_warm_standing reaches it only
//    through a CITED replay counter, extdeps.ninfer.replayed_tokens_total is TranscribedUncited, and
//    this host has no GPU to observe a receipt for. Funding a rate axis would commit the harm that row
//    names — "a card is ranked on a workload it did not actually hold".
//  - Whether the model FITS. The honest verdict is unread, and it is NOT funded as an unread axis on
//    purpose: scan_feasibility answers an unread funded axis with SelectionNeedsEvidence BEFORE the
//    policy is consulted, which would mask the one refusal this fold most needs to show. Reading it
//    would need a bytes-per-token figure for a named engine at a named quantization, and no such
//    figure is cited anywhere in this repo — converting a caller's token count into cache bytes by
//    guesswork is exactly the invented figure §4d refuses. The route is named (a cited
//    bytes-per-token authority, then an AxisGap per unread candidate until it exists) and is left
//    unbuilt rather than stubbed.
//
// THE LIVE ANSWER TODAY is the kernel's own and it is a refusal. The cited figures do not separate
// the field — every card is offered at the same cited rungs — so UniqueSurvivorRequired answers
// SelectionNeedsPolicy naming every survivor. Refusing to choose is the contribution (§3d), and
// §5's "a wrong answer is a loud error, not a warning" rules out picking.
//
// NO FLEET IS ASSERTED. This fold never observes a device and never claims a card was bought,
// installed, or measured. Presence and measurement stay refused in their own lanes.
import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.nat { Nat, nat_to_decimal_string }
import std.measure { Watt, watt, watt_count }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref, NamedField }
import std.interval { degenerate_interval }
import std.pareto { AxisGap, AxisReading, LowerIsBetter, ParetoEntry, SelectionAxis }
import std.decision {
  CausalModelEstablished,
  ChoiceDomain,
  ConstraintSatisfied,
  DecisionAssessment,
  DecisionConstraint,
  DecisionRegion,
  DecisionSubject,
  DecisionVariable,
  ContextParameter,
  HardConstraint,
  NonMoneyConstraint,
  ObjectiveMeasure,
  ObjectiveTerm,
  ParameterBound,
  PhysicalModelEstablished,
  RealizationSelectionResult,
  RelationOtherwiseEstablished,
  SelectionPolicy,
  UniqueSurvivorRequired,
  VariesAlong,
  VendorDocumentedCoupling,
  select_realization,
}
import extdeps.external_authority {
  CitedFigureStanding,
  CitedToAuthority,
  ExternalAuthority,
  FactCitation,
  TranscribedUncited,
}
import extdeps.gpu.types { GpuModelCatalogRow, gpu_catalog_row_eq }
// NOT the four rows. This module used to import each add-in card by name so two hand-written chains could
// key on those names; review 86449 filed that as the second source of the roster. A card now enters the
// sweep only through home_serving_add_in_card_catalog_rows in gunbc.serving.home_serving_accelerator_field,
// so adding a fifth card is one row there and ZERO edits here (§6b: "adding it edits a row, not a branch").
// The datasheet authority stays imported because the exclusion cause names it as what a card must be cited
// to before its ceiling can be trusted.
import extdeps.gpu.nvidia { rtx_pro_6000_workstation_datasheet_authority }
import gunbc.serving.home_serving_accelerator_field {
  home_serving_add_in_card_catalog_rows,
  home_serving_reference_sxm_catalog_rows,
}
import gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy {
  HomeServingPowerCause,
  HomeServingPowerReading,
  PowerPolicyAdmitted,
  PowerPolicyRefused,
  PowerPolicyUnread,
  home_serving_power_policy,
  home_serving_power_admission_applied,
}

data hscf_module: String = "gunbc.serving.home_serving_candidate_field"

// ── IDENTITIES (§3: symbol references, never positions, never re-spelled strings) ────────────
data hscf_field_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_candidate_field")
data home_serving_selection_subject: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_selection_subject")
data home_serving_choice_domain: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_choice_domain")
data home_serving_rung_variable: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_rung_variable")
data home_serving_applied_watt_term: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_applied_watt_term")
data home_serving_objective: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_objective")
data home_serving_region: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_region")
data home_serving_buyer_ceiling_parameter: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_buyer_ceiling_parameter")
data home_serving_unique_survivor_policy: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_unique_survivor_policy")
data home_serving_power_ceiling_authority: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_power_ceiling_authority")
data home_serving_evidence_snapshot: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_evidence_snapshot")
data home_serving_buyer_psu_ceiling: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "home_serving_buyer_psu_ceiling")

// THE ONE FUNDED AXIS: the applied draw of the pair, in watts, LowerIsBetter. It is a fact about the
// pair, not a preference: a lower applied limit under the same cited ceiling is the cheaper, cooler
// home box, so among a card's own admissible rungs the lower draw dominates. Admissibility gets no
// separate axis because the ceiling binds this same axis directly — which is what §3d asks for when
// it says the same Watt cannot be both bound and axis "without saying which". Saying which: bound
// for the buyer's ceiling, axis for the draw that bound measures.
data ax_applied_draw_watts: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: hscf_module, decl_name: "ax_applied_draw_watts")

data home_serving_candidate_axes: List<SelectionAxis> = [
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_applied_draw_watts, goal: LowerIsBetter },
]

// THE RUNGS. "Cards × power limits" is the assigned question, so a sweep list is a P4 input and not
// an invention (the plan: "Power rungs: a sweep list of Watt values, safe to type"). These are the
// APPLIED arm of the option-C pair and are never read as a catalog TDP. The list is of WATT VALUES,
// as the plan words it — not bare counts: a List<Nat> here would put the unit in the list's NAME while
// every consumer had to remember it, which is the unit defect §3 refuses; the carrier is Measure itself
// (std/measure.dag, Watt = Measure<Power, One, Nat>). So a rung cannot be handed to a TDP, a bandwidth,
// or a token count by accident. Counts are WATTS, not milliwatts, so 600 here is the cited TDP of the
// Workstation Edition read off its own row, not a round number chosen to make a case.
// EACH RUNG CARRIES ITS OWN WARRANT. The ladder used to be a bare List<Watt> and the fold invented a
// standing for every rung with one constant function, `hscf_applied_ground(rung) -> TranscribedUncited`.
// That made the shape of this module's answer a fact about CODE: whatever the data said, the policy would
// short-circuit at AppliedStandingUnstated, so the Admitted and Refused arms below were unreachable by
// construction and could never be executed — DESIGN.md "name the consumer AND THE EXECUTING ROUTE" is not
// satisfied by an arm nothing can reach, and a witness claiming "all sixteen come back as gaps" would
// have been vacuously true, proved by a constant rather than by the catalog. Now the standing is DATA
// beside the watt (the pair is the policy module's own HomeServingPowerReading, not a second carrier), and
// an operator who obtains a real power-limit receipt adds one rung row with ObservedByReceipt — the
// comparison runs, over-capacity cells get refused, the rest are admitted, and no branch is edited (§6b).
// All four rungs are TranscribedUncited TODAY because no receipt exists; that is the honest current data,
// not a limitation of the type.
data hscf_planned_rung_standing: CitedFigureStanding = TranscribedUncited {
  read_obligation: ("an applied rung in this sweep is a planned setting for a home tower; grounded only by an operator-applied power-limit receipt on the actual host at that setting (the plan requires no watt without its CitedFigureStanding)"),
}

data home_serving_applied_rungs: List<HomeServingPowerReading> = [
  HomeServingPowerReading { limit: watt(count: 200), standing: hscf_planned_rung_standing },
  HomeServingPowerReading { limit: watt(count: 300), standing: hscf_planned_rung_standing },
  HomeServingPowerReading { limit: watt(count: 400), standing: hscf_planned_rung_standing },
  HomeServingPowerReading { limit: watt(count: 600), standing: hscf_planned_rung_standing },
]


// WHAT THE BUYER STATES, not what the catalog knows. A home tower has one PSU; that ceiling is the
// buyer's own constraint, so it enters as an input carrying its reason rather than as a constant
// hidden in this module. It is a Watt, NOT a bare Nat, and for the same reason the rungs above are:
// the watt has one authority, std.measure::Watt, and a Nat field named for watts carries that same
// fact outside it (§3, "Minimization holds only if each fact lives in exactly one place"). A stated
// intent and a measured figure differ in STANDING, not in unit — the distinction is carried by
// HomeServingPowerStanding, so it never needed to be paid for by dropping the carrier. A Nat here
// would also be handed straight to bound_micro at the constraint boundary with nothing typed saying
// which scale applies (review 86420).
type HomeServingBuyerTerms {
  max_applied_watts: Watt
}

// THE APPLIED ARM'S WARRANT LIVES IN THE RUNG ROWS ABOVE, not here. It used to be
// `fn hscf_applied_ground(rung: Nat) -> CitedFigureStanding = TranscribedUncited { ... }` — one constant
// function that manufactured a standing for whatever watt it was handed. A function that returns the same
// arm for every input is not a reading of the world, it is a decision wired into a branch: it made the
// policy's Admitted/Refused arms unreachable and this fold's verdict a tautology. The standing is data
// (hscf_planned_rung_standing) carried on each rung, and the read_obligation no longer repeats the watt
// the rung already states. Standing it CitedToAuthority would assert a datasheet states a sweep value it
// never mentions (§4d: "do not assert as deduced what is only inferred") — the defect review 79389 filed
// on this lane for the Workstation core count.

// WHY A RENDERER AND NOT A PREDICATE. The policy module deliberately has no
// `..._is_unread(cause) -> Bool`: which arm the result landed in already answers go-read-it versus
// do-not-retry, and a Bool beside the arms is a second answer that can drift (the module's own
// comment says so, and the serving subject module follows the same rule for its axis readings). This
// is not that. It does not re-decide anything — by the time it is called the arm has been decided —
// it renders the closed cause into the `cause` field of a kernel AxisGap so the refusal is
// ATTRIBUTABLE at the instrument surface, which is what the policy carries the cause for ("carried
// for attribution and never interrogated for the split"). Total over the coproduct, no `_` arm, so a
// new cause cannot be swallowed here. Lives in this module, not the policy: the gap vocabulary is
// the consumer's, and the policy knows nothing about Pareto.
fn hscf_unread_gap_cause(cause: HomeServingPowerCause) -> NonEmptyStr =
  match cause {
    TdpUnread => ("card carries no TDP figure at all; the ceiling is unstated, so nothing can be refused against it" as NonEmptyStr)
    TdpStandingUnstated => ("card's TDP is transcribed without a source; cite it to the datasheet or the product page before the ceiling can be trusted" as NonEmptyStr)
    AppliedLimitUnread => ("no applied power limit was offered for this card" as NonEmptyStr)
    AppliedStandingUnstated => ("applied rung is a planned sweep value with no power-limit receipt behind it; go read an operator-applied power-limit receipt on the actual card" as NonEmptyStr)
    AppliedLimitExceedsTdp { applied_watts: applied, tdp_watts: ceiling } =>
      ("applied " + nat_to_decimal_string(value: watt_count(w: applied)) + " W exceeds the card's cited TDP " + nat_to_decimal_string(value: watt_count(w: ceiling)) + " W" as NonEmptyStr)
  }

// ── CANDIDATE IDENTITIES (§3: symbol references, never positions, never re-spelled strings) ────
// ONE FUNCTION MINTS EVERY CELL'S IDENTITY from the two lists this fold is given: the roster (through the
// anchor each row carries in its own citations) and the rung ladder. There is deliberately NO per-cell
// `data` here. There used to be sixteen, and TWO hand-written chains keyed them — `hscf_rung_for`
// re-listing 200/300/400/600 beside the ladder, and `home_serving_candidate_identity` re-listing the four
// row names beside the roster. Review 86449 filed what that costs: add a fifth rung or a fifth card, the
// policy admits the pair, the hand-written lookup answers `Absent`, the builder maps that to `[]`, and the
// cell disappears with no typed cause — and the witness's "sixteen cells" only caught it because sixteen
// was a literal copied out of the tree (§3 "each fact lives in exactly one place"; §6b "adding a case
// edits a row, not a branch"). A computed name is production practice, not a test shortcut:
// gunbc.rust_function_type_selection::position_ref and gunbc.v2_compile_obligation_census::rung_drop_ref
// both mint DeclarationRefs whose decl_name a function builds.
// The names stay self-describing (`cand_<row>_at_<n>w`) because a reader greps for them; they are simply
// no longer written down twice. There is no "unknown rung" arm any more because the only source of rungs
// is the ladder this function is called over — an `Absent` for a watt outside a hand-listed set existed
// only to serve the hand-written table it looked up in.
fn home_serving_cell_identity(anchor: String, rung: Watt) -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(
    module_path: hscf_module,
    decl_name: "cand_" + anchor + "_at_" + nat_to_decimal_string(value: watt_count(w: rung)) + "w",
  )
}

// THE ROW'S ANCHOR AND ITS CEILING, TAKEN FROM THE ONE ENTRY THAT RECORDS BOTH. Every
// figure_citations row on these four cards is anchored at the card's own declaration (checked: 5/5, 5/5,
// 5/5 and 4/4 entries respectively), so the `tdp_watts` citation states both WHERE the figure came from
// and WHICH ROW it is a figure of. Reading the row's identity out of that citation instead of a chain of
// `gpu_catalog_row_eq` calls against hand-typed strings is what keeps a datasheet rename honest: the cell
// names follow the row rather than drifting from a copy in this module (§3, "each fact lives in exactly
// one place"). The old chain is what review 86449 filed as the second source.
// WHY ONE LOOKUP AND NOT TWO. Asking separately for the authority and for the row's name would be two
// traversals that could disagree — a citation found by one and a name matched by the other is precisely
// the drift the reviewer asked me to remove. So the pair travels together, and the carrier lives HERE
// rather than in the policy module: it is this fold's concern which row a cell belongs to, and the policy
// knows nothing about candidate fields. The figure and the standing travel together inside
// HomeServingPowerReading, so no caller can take the number and stand it by something friendlier (§4d).
// WHAT A MIS-ANCHORED CITATION DOES: the two cells would come back with the SAME identity, and std.pareto
// refuses a field carrying duplicate identities rather than ranking them. A copy-pasted citation is
// therefore loud at the kernel, not silently re-labelled.
type HomeServingCellWarrant {
  anchor: String
  tdp: HomeServingPowerReading
}

// WHY A CAUSE AND NOT AN `Absent`. Two different facts stop a cell here and they need different fixes:
// the row publishes NO tdp_watts figure at all (TdpUnread — go find a datasheet that states one), versus
// the row publishes a figure with nothing citing it (TdpStandingUnstated — the number exists, its warrant
// does not). Collapsing them into one `Absent` would send a reader looking for a datasheet that already
// exists. The causes are the policy module's own (§6: no second vocabulary for the same question).
type HomeServingWarrantRead
  = WarrantRead { warrant: HomeServingCellWarrant }
  | WarrantMissing { cause: HomeServingPowerCause }

fn hscf_cell_warrant(row: GpuModelCatalogRow) -> HomeServingWarrantRead {
  match row.tdp_watts {
    Absent => WarrantMissing { cause: TdpUnread }
    Present { value: tdp } =>
      match hscf_find_tdp_citation(citations: row.figure_citations) {
        Absent => WarrantMissing { cause: TdpStandingUnstated }
        Present { value: citation } =>
          WarrantRead {
            warrant: HomeServingCellWarrant {
              anchor: citation.fact.decl_name as String,
              tdp: HomeServingPowerReading { limit: tdp, standing: CitedToAuthority { authority: citation.authority } },
            },
          }
      }
  }
}

fn hscf_find_tdp_citation(citations: List<FactCitation>) -> FactCitation? {
  fold(
    citations,
    init: Absent,
    f: (found, c) =>
      match found {
        Present { value: already } => Present { value: already }
        Absent =>
          match c.fact.field {
            NamedField { field_name: f } =>
              if f == ("tdp_watts" as NonEmptyStr) {
                Present { value: c }
              } else {
                Absent
              }
            _ => Absent
          }
      },
  )
}

// THE LABEL carries the watt figure it stands for (§13620: "a bare candidate label cannot carry the
// fact" — the failure where a later reader takes a label for a measurement). The figure lives in the
// label AND in the derived identity's name, so neither can be read as the whole fact. TOTAL, with no
// roster lookup: the label is made of what the row itself states plus the rung it is offered at, so there
// is no longer an `Absent` path for it to fall through.
fn home_serving_candidate_label(row: GpuModelCatalogRow, limit: Watt) -> NonEmptyStr {
  (row.model + " @ " + nat_to_decimal_string(value: watt_count(w: limit)) + " W applied") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE OUTCOME OF ONE CELL, AND WHY IT IS NOT `ParetoEntry?`. Review 86449 filed the defect this type
// removes: the minter used to answer `Absent` for three different situations (the row's TDP carries no
// citation, the applied rung is over that card's own cited ceiling, the roster does not recognise the
// row) and the field builder turned every one of them into `Absent => []`. Three distinct facts collapsed
// into one vanished cell with no typed cause — §5: "A failure arm refuses, never widens, and every
// degradation is typed, located and countable", and §3d: "absence is not irrelevance". So the minter is
// now TOTAL and says which of the three it is. Excluded cells stay out of the field (std.decision
// scan_feasibility excludes a definite violation before Pareto; §3d forbids ranking one), but they are
// NAMEABLE: home_serving_candidate_exclusions returns them, located by the roster row and the rung, with
// the policy module's own HomeServingPowerCause as the cause — no second cause vocabulary (§6).
type HomeServingCellExclusion {
  row: GpuModelCatalogRow
  rung: Watt
  cause: HomeServingPowerCause
}

type HomeServingCellOutcome
  = CellOffered { entry: ParetoEntry }
  | CellExcluded { exclusion: HomeServingCellExclusion }

// THE ONE ENTRY MINTER, and the order is the whole design:
//  1. the row must carry a TDP WITH the citation that grounds it, else the cell is excluded (no invented
//     ceiling — the same shape P1 chose for an unstated lane count, extdeps.gpu.machine_shape
//     CatalogExecutionLanesUnstated);
//  2. gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy must refuse the pair if the applied watt is above that
//     card's cited TDP — a claimed operating point that breaks the card's own ceiling is not a candidate
//     at all (front-door refusal, before Pareto, never a ranked entry);
//  3. a pair whose watt is UNREAD is offered WITH its gap (see below), not dropped;
//  4. only an ADMITTED pair becomes a ParetoEntry with a reading on the funded axis.
// WHAT THE CELLS DO TODAY, stated exactly because the count is what a reader will check: the ladder
// currently carries no power-limit receipt, so the policy's standing gate returns Unread before it ever
// compares, and every cell of the sweep comes back OFFERED-WITH-A-GAP with nothing excluded. That split
// (offered + excluded = the product of the two lists) is claimed, from the lists, in
// test.claim.home_serving_candidate_field_witness::home_serving_offered_plus_excluded_is_the_whole_product —
// not by a literal copied out of the tree. The arms this function can also return are executed there by
// data the catalog already holds: home_serving_row_without_a_tdp_is_excluded_with_its_cause excludes the
// real B200 SXM row (its tdp_watts is none), and
// home_serving_comparison_arms_are_reachable_on_one_card runs both comparison arms over the RTX PRO 5000
// 48 GB row's own cited 300 W ceiling (200 W admitted, 400 W refused) using probe rungs whose standing is
// cited — the same probe convention test.claim.home_serving_power_policy_witness already uses for the
// policy module, and NOT a claim that any datasheet states an applied sweep value.
// WHY THE RUNG ARRIVES AS A READING AND NOT A WATT. The fold hands this function the rung row from
// WHY THE RUNG ARRIVES AS A READING AND NOT A WATT. The fold hands this function the rung row from
// home_serving_applied_rungs — limit AND standing together — so the comparison the policy performs is
// between two figures each with its own warrant. Taking a bare Watt here and inventing the standing inside
// (the old `hscf_applied_ground`) is what made the Admitted and Refused arms unreachable: a constant
// standing is a decision wired into a branch instead of a fact read from data. Probes that need to see a
// grounded comparison run — over-capacity refusal, admitted selection — pass a rung row carrying
// ObservedByReceipt through the same function, which is how those two arms get an executing route
// (DESIGN.md: every added declaration names its consumer AND its executing route).
fn home_serving_candidate_entry(row: GpuModelCatalogRow, rung: HomeServingPowerReading) -> HomeServingCellOutcome {
  let limit: Watt = rung.limit
  match hscf_cell_warrant(row: row) {
    // A row that cannot lend a GROUNDED number to the ceiling is excluded, with the policy's own cause
    // saying which of the two it is: no figure at all, or a figure nobody cites. Both are reported rather
    // than answered `Absent`, because an excluded cell and a vanished cell are different facts and only a
    // nameable one can be chased down. Neither mints an entry whose axis value nobody stated (§4d).
    WarrantMissing { cause: cause } =>
      CellExcluded { exclusion: HomeServingCellExclusion { row: row, rung: limit, cause: cause } }
    WarrantRead { warrant: warrant } =>
      match home_serving_power_policy(
        tdp: Present { value: warrant.tdp },
        applied: Present { value: rung },
      ) {
        PowerPolicyAdmitted { admission: admitted } =>
          CellOffered {
            entry: ParetoEntry {
              identity: home_serving_cell_identity(anchor: warrant.anchor, rung: home_serving_power_admission_applied(admission: admitted)),
              display_label: home_serving_candidate_label(row: row, limit: home_serving_power_admission_applied(admission: admitted)),
              readings: [
                AxisReading {
                  axis: ax_applied_draw_watts,
                  interval_micro: degenerate_interval(point: watt_count(w: home_serving_power_admission_applied(admission: admitted)) as Int),
                },
              ],
              missing_inputs: [],
            },
          }
        // REFUSED means the pair can never be a candidate: the applied watt is a claimed operating point
        // that breaks the card's own cited ceiling, and §3d excludes it before Pareto, never ranks it. It
        // is reported, not silently absent — see the exclusion type above.
        PowerPolicyRefused { cause: cause } =>
          CellExcluded { exclusion: HomeServingCellExclusion { row: row, rung: limit, cause: cause } }
        // UNREAD IS NOT REFUSED, and dropping the cell here was the defect this comment now forbids.
        // The rung is a planned sweep value with no power-limit receipt behind it yet; the card and the
        // rung are both known, so the candidate EXISTS and its watt is UNREAD — two different facts.
        // Returning Absent handed the kernel an empty field, and the kernel then said
        // "candidate field is empty" (std.decision establish_candidate_field), which is the arm for
        // nobody having offered a candidate at all. Silencing known candidates into that arm is the
        // substitution §5 rejects: std.pareto's own rule for a ParetoEntry is that a nonempty
        // missing_inputs "states what is absent instead of substituting a zero or an average", and
        // scan_feasibility answers a declared gap with SelectionNeedsEvidence carrying the cause. The
        // candidate is therefore offered WITH its gap, and the instrument's own refusal — go read the
        // receipt — is what a caller sees.
        PowerPolicyUnread { cause: c } =>
          CellOffered {
            entry: ParetoEntry {
              identity: home_serving_cell_identity(anchor: warrant.anchor, rung: limit),
              display_label: home_serving_candidate_label(row: row, limit: limit),
              readings: [],
              missing_inputs: [AxisGap { axis: ax_applied_draw_watts, cause: hscf_unread_gap_cause(cause: c) }],
            },
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE TWO PROJECTIONS OF THAT ONE MINTER. Nothing decides here: both walk the same product of the same
// two lists with the same pure function, so they cannot disagree with each other or with the minter —
// the partition is a property of one function applied once per cell, which is what lets the witness
// claim offered + excluded = |roster| x |ladder| without trusting a count typed into a test file.
fn home_serving_candidate_field() -> List<ParetoEntry> {
  flat_map(home_serving_add_in_card_catalog_rows, row =>
    flat_map(home_serving_applied_rungs, rung =>
      // The rung is passed as the RECORD, warrant included. Handing the policy a watt and letting this
      // module supply a standing was the defect the minter's comment above documents.
      match home_serving_candidate_entry(row: row, rung: rung) {
        CellOffered { entry: entry } => [entry]
        CellExcluded { exclusion: _ } => []
      },
    ),
  )
}

fn home_serving_candidate_exclusions() -> List<HomeServingCellExclusion> {
  flat_map(home_serving_add_in_card_catalog_rows, row =>
    flat_map(home_serving_applied_rungs, rung =>
      match home_serving_candidate_entry(row: row, rung: rung) {
        CellOffered { entry: _ } => []
        CellExcluded { exclusion: exclusion } => [exclusion]
      },
    ),
  )
}

// THE UNIVERSAL CEILING, AS THE KERNEL'S OWN CONSTRAINT. This bound is honest as a HardConstraint
// precisely because it applies to every candidate alike. Expressing it here rather than as an
// if-statement in the field builder keeps exclusion in ONE authority (std.decision
// scan_feasibility): a buyer ceiling below every rung comes back as NoFeasibleRealization carrying the
// kernel's causes, not as a silently empty list.
fn home_serving_buyer_ceiling_constraint(terms: HomeServingBuyerTerms) -> HardConstraint = HardConstraint {
  identity: home_serving_buyer_psu_ceiling,
  axis: ax_applied_draw_watts,
  bound_micro: watt_count(w: terms.max_applied_watts) as Int,
  denomination: NonMoneyConstraint,
}

fn home_serving_constraints(terms: HomeServingBuyerTerms) -> List<HardConstraint> {
  [home_serving_buyer_ceiling_constraint(terms: terms)]
}

// THE ASSESSMENT: one subject, one context parameter (the buyer's ceiling), one variable (the rung),
// one objective term (applied draw), one constraint record. Physical and causal models are
// ESTABLISHED, not deferred: the cost relation is the vendor's own datasheet statement, the term
// varies along the rung, and the constraint disposition is Satisfied because no entry reaches the
// field without passing the power policy's applied ≤ cited TDP rule — the completeness argument that
// policy's own witness makes, referenced rather than re-derived (§2).
//
// Deferring here would not be caution, it would be a bug: std.decision merge_deferred_into_pick
// rewrites a genuine SelectionNeedsPolicy into SelectionNeedsEvidence under a Deferred closure, so a
// fake hedge would erase the near-miss refusal this fold exists to produce.
fn home_serving_power_assessment(terms: HomeServingBuyerTerms) -> DecisionAssessment {
  DecisionAssessment {
    subject: DecisionSubject {
      identity: home_serving_selection_subject,
      question: "which cited add-in card, at which applied power limit, is the home serving candidate" as NonEmptyStr,
      parameters: [ContextParameter { identity: home_serving_buyer_ceiling_parameter }],
      constraints: [DecisionConstraint { identity: home_serving_power_ceiling_authority }],
      objective: ObjectiveMeasure {
        identity: home_serving_objective,
        goal: LowerIsBetter,
        terms: [ObjectiveTerm { identity: home_serving_applied_watt_term, ground: CitedToAuthority { authority: rtx_pro_6000_workstation_datasheet_authority } }],
      },
      choice_domain: ChoiceDomain {
        identity: home_serving_choice_domain,
        variables: [DecisionVariable { identity: home_serving_rung_variable }],
      },
    },
    sensitivities: [
      VariesAlong {
        term: home_serving_applied_watt_term,
        variable: home_serving_rung_variable,
        region: DecisionRegion { identity: home_serving_region },
        relation: RelationOtherwiseEstablished { note: "the applied rung sets what a card is asked to hold and is bounded by that card's cited TDP; it is not evidence of throughput, which this fold may not fund until a WarmValid exists" as NonEmptyStr },
        coupling: CitedToAuthority { authority: rtx_pro_6000_workstation_datasheet_authority },
      },
    ],
    constraints: [
      ConstraintSatisfied {
        constraint: home_serving_power_ceiling_authority,
        evidence: "every entry in the field was admitted by gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy, which refuses applied > cited TDP at minting; a row whose TDP carries no citation contributes no entry at all" as NonEmptyStr,
      },
    ],
    parameters: [
      ParameterBound {
        parameter: home_serving_buyer_ceiling_parameter,
        evidence: "the buyer's PSU ceiling is a stated input, expressed as a std.decision HardConstraint on the funded axis rather than a filter beside it" as NonEmptyStr,
      },
    ],
    selected_candidate: Absent,
    term_drops: [],
    physical_model: PhysicalModelEstablished { terms: [home_serving_applied_watt_term] },
    causal_model: CausalModelEstablished {
      couplings: [
        VendorDocumentedCoupling {
          authority: rtx_pro_6000_workstation_datasheet_authority,
          term: home_serving_applied_watt_term,
          variable: home_serving_rung_variable,
          subject: home_serving_selection_subject,
        },
      ],
    },
  }
}

// THE FOLD. Everything after this call is std.decision's: establishment, the feasibility scan and its
// exclusion of definite violations, domination, the policy, and the receipt. This module re-implements
// none of it (§2: "net concepts must not grow by re-invention"; §3d: "select_realization is the ONLY
// decision fold"). The field is a parameter for the same reason the flagship fold takes its routes as
// evidence: a caller who has read a power-limit receipt, or who can state a working set, hands in the
// better field rather than this module hard-coding what the field may contain.
fn select_home_serving_candidate(
  field: List<ParetoEntry>,
  terms: HomeServingBuyerTerms,
  policy: SelectionPolicy,
) -> RealizationSelectionResult<ParetoEntry> {
  select_realization(
    assessment: home_serving_power_assessment(terms: terms),
    candidate_field_identity: hscf_field_identity,
    field: field,
    axes: home_serving_candidate_axes,
    constraints: home_serving_constraints(terms: terms),
    constraint_authority: home_serving_power_ceiling_authority,
    policy: policy,
    evidence_snapshot: home_serving_evidence_snapshot,
  )
}

// THE LIVE ANSWER TODAY, kept as the fold's own entry point so a reader sees what the current field
// actually returns rather than a hand-picked case. UniqueSurvivorRequired is the policy an operator
// ruling cannot replace with a pick: when cards survive with no stated preference the kernel answers
// SelectionNeedsPolicy and names every survivor.
fn home_serving_selection_for(terms: HomeServingBuyerTerms) -> RealizationSelectionResult<ParetoEntry> {
  select_home_serving_candidate(
    field: home_serving_candidate_field(),
    terms: terms,
    policy: UniqueSurvivorRequired { identity: home_serving_unique_survivor_policy },
  )
}

// THE FRONTIER THIS MODULE FIRES. SXM rows are references, so they reach this module to be asserted
// NON-candidate rather than to be ignored: if they were simply unused, "reference only" would be a
// comment. Their absence from the field is also claimed in the witness, where it can go red.
fn home_serving_sxm_rows_are_not_candidates() -> Bool {
  all(home_serving_reference_sxm_catalog_rows, row =>
    !any(home_serving_add_in_card_catalog_rows, add_in => gpu_catalog_row_eq(a: row, b: add_in))
  )
}
